WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.770
Some counting problems are too big to list,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.830
but they grow in a regular way.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.040
If you can explain how each case is built from smaller

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.310
ones, you can climb to any size.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.770
That building rule is called a recurrence.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.030
A strip of ten cells can be tiled in eighty-nine ways.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.850
Nobody wants to list those.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.070
A recurrence gets the answer with nine small additions.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.190
A strip is covered using tiles of length one and tiles

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.180
of length two.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.100
A strip of length one can be tiled in one way,

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.280
length two in two ways, length three in three ways,

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:34.840
length four in five ways.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.360
How many ways are there for length five?

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.860
Pause and look at how a tiling ends.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.040
Look at the last tile.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.150
Either it is a single, and the rest is a tiling

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.020
of length four.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.910
Or it is a double, and the rest is a tiling

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:52.820
of length three.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.300
Every tiling ends in exactly one of these two ways.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.710
So the number for length five is the number for length

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.900
four plus the number for length three.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:03.800
Five plus three is eight.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:05.470
Keep going.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.330
Eight plus five is thirteen.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.390
Then twenty-one, thirty-four, fifty-five, eighty-nine.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.370
Each number is the sum of the two before it.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.000
The same rule appears in disguise.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.930
Climbing stairs one or two steps at a time is the same

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:24.400
problem: the last move is a single step or a double.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.120
Six stairs can be climbed in thirteen ways.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.490
Sort the possibilities by their last move.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.950
Each group matches a smaller case.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.030
Add the groups.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.800
Notebook challenge: how many ways are there to tile a

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.230
strip of length seven with singles and doubles?
